{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>וְנֶעְלַם מִמֶּנּוּ וְהוּא טָמֵא</b> — that the tum'ah was hidden from him. <b>וְאָשֵׁם</b> — in the eating of consecrated things or in entry to the Sanctuary, in the language of Rashi (Rashi on Leviticus 5:2). But the intention is not that eating consecrated things and entry to the Sanctuary are derived from \"וְאָשֵׁם,\" for this is stated in all the offerings above. Rather, this passage abbreviates what is self-understood, for since there is no sin whatsoever in touching a carcass (נְבֵלָה) or a creeping thing (שֶׁרֶץ), and they were not warned against it even the priests, it is impossible that Scripture would obligate a sacrifice for touching them. But it said that when a person becomes impure and the tum'ah was hidden from him, or when he takes an oath and the oath was hidden from him, and he sins through the concealment of either one of these, he becomes obligated to bring a sacrifice. And it is known that there is no sin in the concealment of tum'ah other than that he eat consecrated things or enter the Sanctuary, and there is no sin in the concealment of an oath other than that he transgress it. And this is the plain sense of the verses in this passage. And our Sages have further midrashim in it to reinforce this matter:"], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 5, :notes {0 "The closing colon in the source suggests Ramban is about to cite rabbinic derivations; the translation preserves the colon as it appears."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:50:31.294644305Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}